qfagent (0.1.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Report WAN throughput in DeviceStats (rx_bps/tx_bps, proto fields 13/14):
    per-interface rx/tx byte-counter deltas between ~30 s snapshots, summed
    across the WAN-facing interfaces and converted to bits/sec. WAN membership
    comes from the firewall's own config — the "WAN" zone's members (name or
    [dn:WAN] display name), else the "WAN" interface-group alias — falling
    back to the kernel's default-route interface(s). First snapshot after
    start sends 0/0 ("not measured" to the controller); counter wraps/resets
    clamp to zero. Older controllers ignore the unknown fields.

 -- QuartzFire <cwellman@quartz.systems>  Wed, 22 Jul 2026 12:00:00 +0000

qfagent (0.1.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Push periodic DeviceStats up the existing ControlStream (~30 s, alongside
    SecurityTelemetry): CPU / memory / disk utilization gauges, uptime, the
    device's outbound (WAN) address, and the busiest firewall rules by bytes
    from the nftables vyos_filter counters. Fire-and-forget, like the security
    telemetry; any unavailable source degrades to a zero/empty field rather
    than dropping the whole snapshot. Adds DeviceStats/PolicyStat (field 4) to
    the device.proto contract.

 -- QuartzFire <cwellman@quartz.systems>  Mon, 20 Jul 2026 15:00:00 +0000

qfagent (0.1.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the QuartzCommand control channel being refused after every image
    upgrade ("identity/host mismatch — run 'qf identity regenerate'").
    Clone-detection recorded /etc/machine-id as a host fact and treated any
    change as a cloned image. But on VyOS each installed image regenerates
    its own machine-id (systemd, first boot) while the device identity
    persists in the cross-image `/config` — so `add system image` + reboot
    tripped the gate on the SAME hardware and forced a needless identity
    regenerate + re-enroll. machine-id is now recorded for diagnostics only;
    clone detection gates solely on the DMI product UUID, the real
    hardware anchor that is stable across image upgrades.

 -- QuartzFire <cwellman@quartz.systems>  Mon, 20 Jul 2026 12:00:00 +0000

qfagent (0.1.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix enrollment failing at the final step with "rename into
    /run/qfagent/scrub-request: Is a directory": the scrub path unit's
    MakeDirectory=yes created the watched trigger path ITSELF as a
    directory at boot, so the conf-mode owner's temp+rename bump failed,
    enrollment state was never persisted and the control channel never
    started (device adopted controller-side but shown offline / "Not
    enrolled" locally). Dropped MakeDirectory= (qfagent.service's
    RuntimeDirectory= already creates /run/qfagent) and made the trigger
    bump remove a stray directory left by 0.1.0 so upgraded boxes recover
    without a reboot.

 -- QuartzFire <cwellman@quartz.systems>  Sun, 19 Jul 2026 12:00:00 +0000

qfagent (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release: QuartzCommand cloud enrollment for QuartzFire.
    - Ed25519 device identity under /config/quartzfire/qfagent/identity/
      (device ID QF-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX derived from the public key), with
      machine-id + DMI host fingerprinting for clone protection and a
      reserved TPM keying seam.
    - `system quartz-command` config nodes (gateway, port, ca-certificate,
      one-shot enroll-token); committing a token enrolls synchronously and a
      root one-shot then scrubs the token from the config and persists the
      token's gateway.
    - Enrollment TLS trust: WebPKI first, token CA-fingerprint pinning as
      fallback; the pinned CA is persisted for the control channel.
    - Persistent mTLS control channel (exponential backoff with jitter,
      25 s HTTP/2 keepalive) and certificate renewal at 2/3 lifetime with a
      <7-day alarm.
    - `show quartz-command status` op-mode command and the `qf` CLI
      (status / identity regenerate / prepare-template).

 -- QuartzFire <cwellman@quartz.systems>  Sun, 19 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
